  couldn't follow him there so that was  made it that a non-viable strategy  you know of course that in the summer of  1970 we did make one foray into cambodia  u.s and vietnamese forces together and  it was very successful in its own terms  a lot of military wherewithal was  scarfed up and that clearly hindered the  enemy's ability to mount a major  offensive for quite a time maybe a year  until he could build those  those stockpiles back up again but at  the time we did it it was limited by how  deep it could go and by how long it was  going to last and the president  announced both of those to the enemy  right at the beginning so even when we  did it it was done  at the political level  maybe not half-heartedly but something  less than robustly i think that those  limitations however  right or wrong they may have been in  political terms were militarily quite  disadvantageous  yes i want to take one more question  please  do you have any information information  about the retribution that north vietnam  wreaked on the south after their  conquest or was that beyond the scope of  your blog no i i do treat that briefly  in the in the last chapter and in the  in the epilogue the degree  it's uh it's as sad as anybody might  have thought it was going to be  you know of course that at the at the  end of the war we uh  indelible picture of the last helicopter  leaving the roof of the american embassy  we withdrew all the americans  and 130 000 vietnamese thought to be at  risk of people  and their families  and then  hundreds of thousands of more maybe a  million in total  became so-called boat people  sought to flee their own country  tens of thousands more were put into  so-called re-education camps many never  came out alive  others were killed for their opposition  i don't think anybody knows what the  real  whole numbers are  but as a consequence of the viciousness  of the so-called liberators  after they had conquered south vietnam  there is now  what i call a new vietnamese diaspora of  maybe two million  vietnamese living abroad about a million  of them in the united states  some of them are my friends and i know  some of them are friends of many of you  that i see him here in the room tonight  and and i would like to say this too  they have been a very  positive contribution to our country to  its economy and to its culture and we we  are lucky to have them now as countrymen  but you still have to feel very sad for  them that they were essentially driven  from their own country because they  refused to live under tyranny  ladies and gentlemen thank you again for  coming this has been a great pleasure  and i'd be glad to sign your books  [Applause]  [Music]  lewis sorley is a third generation  graduate of west point he spent 20 years  in the u s army he is also taught at  west point and the army war college  later he was a senior civilian official  of the central intelligence agency  mr sorley's book a better war was  published by harcourt brace  found on the internet at harcourt.com  history on book tv is shown every  saturday here on c-span 2 at 8 and 11

